 3. 💡 Last-minute advice 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Andrea Chronopoulos for The New York Times The lead story of today's New York Times business section is time-capsule-ready: "How to Talk With Your Family About A.I. Doom at Thanksgiving." San Francisco-based tech reporter Emmy Martin talked to AI researchers about what they'll bring up and how they'll parry questions.

 Daniel Kokotajlo   — a former OpenAI governance researcher who leads the  AI Futures Project , known for the widely read " AI 2027 " — feels obligated to say something at dinner with his wife's family in Boston. "It feels like maybe I'm doing something wrong if I let the social awkwardness prevent me from bringing it up," said Kokotajlo, 34. 

 Around 2020, he gave the family a presentation on how quickly AI was improving. Now he thinks next year's holiday season will be "one with lots of AI-powered euphoria and whole new gadgets." (Don't ask about later years.) Read the story (gift link). 🎙️ Another piece in today's Times rounds up thoughts from the paper's journalists about how they navigate polarizing topics at holiday gatherings.

 Michael Barbaro, host of "The Daily," said: "I've definitely become my family's receptacle for anger at The Times." White House correspondent Erica L. Green offered an all-purpose riposte: "Who wants another drink?!" Read the story (gift link). 

 
 
